# 🗺️ User Skill: Give Your Own Code an Address in Mathlib

**Audience:** complete beginners. No knowledge of the Monster group, moonshine,
or the rest of this project is required.

This is the *user manual* for the tool in
[`RequestProject/UserAddress.lean`](RequestProject/UserAddress.lean). It lets you
take **your own** definitions, theorems, or domain-specific language and stamp
each one with a short **address** — a coordinate that lives in the **same space
as every declaration in Mathlib**.

---

## TL;DR — the question you asked

> *Can we assign our user-space code (or our own domain language) an address in
> the Mathlib space?*

**Yes.** Every Lean declaration — yours or Mathlib's — gets turned into a number
(its *structural fingerprint*) and then into a triple of coordinates

```
address = (k mod 71, k mod 59, k mod 47)
```

Because `47 · 59 · 71 = 196883`, the Chinese Remainder Theorem guarantees this
triple is a **unique label** for every fingerprint below `196883`. Mathlib uses
the exact same recipe, so your code and Mathlib share one coordinate system, and
you can ask *"which Mathlib lemma sits at my address?"*

This uniqueness is **proved in Lean** (`UserAddress.sameAddress_unique`,
`UserAddress.crtTriple_inj`); there is no `sorry`.

---

## 1. Install / import

In any Lean file in this project, just write:

```lean
import RequestProject.UserAddress
```

That brings three commands into scope: `#address`, `#compareaddress`, `#ward`.

---

## 2. The three commands (with copy-paste examples)

### `#address f` — "what is my address?"

Point it at *any* declaration and it prints an **address card**.

```lean
#address Nat.add        -- a Mathlib function
#address List.map       -- a Mathlib function
```

Output looks like:

```
[Your code @ Mathlib address] Nat.add
  ├── structural fingerprint k = 488
  ├── page slot (k mod 196883) = 488
  ├── address (mod 71, 59, 47) = (62, 16, 18)
  └── this is a valid cell of the 71×59×47 Monster-irrep grid
```

It works identically on **your own** code:

```lean
def myGreeting (n : Nat) : Nat := n + 41
#address myGreeting
```

### `#compareaddress f g` — "how does my code line up with Mathlib?"

Put two declarations side by side and see which coordinates they share:

```lean
#compareaddress myGreeting Nat.add
```

```
[Address comparison]
  myGreeting: (7, 31, 8)
  Nat.add:    (62, 16, 18)
  mod 71: 7 ≠ 62
  mod 59: 31 ≠ 16
  mod 47: 8 ≠ 18
  → different cells; only the coordinates marked (shared) coincide.
```

If all three coordinates match it tells you the two land on the **same grid
cell** — the strongest form of "your code lives where this Mathlib lemma lives".

### `#ward f` — "who are my Mathlib neighbours?"

Scan the loaded environment (Mathlib included) and list a few declarations in the
same **mod-47 ward** (they share your third coordinate):

```lean
#ward myGreeting
```

```
[mod-47 ward of myGreeting]  ward index c47 = 8
  (scanned 817 declarations, showing 12 neighbours)
  • Matrix.smul_hadamard        (type fingerprint 666, 8 mod 47)
  • Finset.subsetSum_mono       (type fingerprint 243, 8 mod 47)
  • finite_irreducible          (type fingerprint 196, 8 mod 47)
  ...
```

These really are Mathlib declarations — proof that your code now shares
neighbourhoods with the standard library.

